<body>
    <div class="header">
        <h1>Global Smartwatch Secondary Market Analysis</h1>
        <div class="subtitle">Q1 2026 — 3,607+ Resale Listings Across Apple, Garmin & Samsung</div>
        <div class="meta">Prepared for: E-Commerce Strategy Analysts | Data Period: Q1 2026</div>
    </div>

    <div class="container">
        <!-- Executive Summary with KPIs -->
        <div class="kpi-grid">
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">{total_listings:,}</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Total Active Listings</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">${median_by_brand["Garmin"]:.0f}</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Garmin Median Price (Highest)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">${geo_spread["Garmin"]:.0f}</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Garmin Geographic Spread</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">{power_seller_concentration:.1f}%</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Power Seller Concentration (Top 5%)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">{case_corr:.3f}</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Case Size-Price Correlation</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Market Overview -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>1. Market Overview & Listing Composition</h2>

            <div class="two-col">
                <div>
                    <h3>Dataset Summary</h3>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Total Listings</span>
                        <strong>{total_listings:,}</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Unique Sellers</span>
                        <strong>{unique_sellers:,}</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Countries Represented</span>
                        <strong>{len(unique_countries)}</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Brands Analyzed</span>
                        <strong>Apple, Garmin, Samsung (+ others)</strong>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Brand Distribution</h3>
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <tr><th>Brand</th><th>Listings</th><th>Share</th></tr>
                        <tr><td>Apple</td><td>2,370</td><td>65.7%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Garmin</td><td>746</td><td>20.7%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Samsung</td><td>470</td><td>13.0%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Others</td><td>21</td><td>0.6%</td></tr>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['brand_dist']}" alt="Brand Distribution">
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Market Composition:</strong> Apple dominates the secondary market with nearly two-thirds of all listings, followed by Garmin at 21%. Samsung holds a 13% share. The high Apple volume reflects both strong brand recognition and a robust ecosystem of trade-in programs.
            </div>

            <h3>Geographic Distribution</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['top_countries']}" alt="Top Countries">
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Geographic Concentration:</strong> The United States dominates with 65% of all listings (2,344), followed by the United Kingdom (13%) and South Korea (6%). This concentration suggests pricing in these markets is most competitive.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 2: Brand Pricing Analysis -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>2. Brand-Level Pricing & Value Retention</h2>

            <div class="two-col">
                <div>
                    <h3>Price Statistics by Brand</h3>
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <tr><th>Brand</th><th>Median</th><th>Mean</th><th>Std Dev</th><th>Range</th></tr>
                        <tr><td>Garmin</td><td>${median_by_brand["Garmin"]:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Garmin', 'mean']:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Garmin', 'std']:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Garmin', 'min']:.0f} - ${brand_stats.loc['Garmin', 'max']:.0f}</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Apple</td><td>${median_by_brand["Apple"]:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Apple', 'mean']:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Apple', 'std']:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Apple', 'min']:.0f} - ${brand_stats.loc['Apple', 'max']:.0f}</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Samsung</td><td>${median_by_brand["Samsung"]:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Samsung', 'mean']:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Samsung', 'std']:.2f}</td><td>${brand_stats.loc['Samsung', 'min']:.0f} - ${brand_stats.loc['Samsung', 'max']:.0f}</td></tr>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['median_price']}" alt="Median Price by Brand">
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <h3>Key Finding: Garmin Commands Highest Median Price</h3>
                <p>Garmin leads with a median price of ${median_by_brand["Garmin"]:.2f}, slightly ahead of Apple (${median_by_brand["Apple"]:.2f}) and Samsung (${median_by_brand["Samsung"]:.2f}). This suggests Garmin watches retain value exceptionally well in the secondary market — likely driven by their appeal to fitness enthusiasts and outdoor users who prioritize functionality over fashion.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box warning">
                <strong>High Variance Alert:</strong> Apple shows the highest standard deviation (${brand_stats.loc['Apple', 'std']:.2f}) and widest price range, indicating significant model-tier differentiation. High-end Apple Watch Ultra listings can reach $1,000+, while older Series 3 models fall below $200.
            </div>

            <h3>New vs. Used Condition Premium</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['new_used']}" alt="New vs Used Premium">
            </div>
            <p>Samsung commands the highest new-vs-used premium at {new_used_premium['Samsung']:.1f}%, followed by Apple at {new_used_premium['Apple']:.1f}% and Garmin at {new_used_premium['Garmin']:.1f}%. This suggests Samsung buyers in the secondary market place a higher premium on condition, possibly due to faster model refresh cycles.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 3: Geographic Pricing Arbitrage (HIGH PRIORITY) -->
        <div class="section" style="border: 3px solid #007AFF;">
            <h2 style="border-bottom-color: #FF9500;">3. Geographic Pricing Arbitrage <span class="tag high">HIGH VALUE</span></h2>

            <p>This section reveals where margin opportunities exist through geographic price discrepancies. The heatmap below shows price deviations from global mean by country and brand.</p>

            <h3>Geographic Price Deviation Heatmap (% from Mean)</h3>
            <div style="overflow-x: auto;">
                {generate_geo_heatmap_html()}
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box success">
                <strong>Arbitrage Opportunity:</strong> Garmin shows the widest geographic spread at ${geo_spread['Garmin']:.0f}, presenting the largest arbitrage potential. Garmin devices tend to be priced significantly above average in Australia (+41%), United Kingdom (+23%), and South Korea (+29%), while being priced below average in Estados Unidos (Mexico, -21%) and Hong Kong (-13%).
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['geo_bar']}" alt="Geographic Price Comparison">
            </div>

            <h3>Country-Level Price Analysis</h3>
            <table class="data-table">
                <tr><th>Country</th><th>Apple Median</th><th>Garmin Median</th><th>Samsung Median</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Australia</td><td>$792.00</td><td>$1,005.03</td><td>$689.00</td></tr>
                <tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>$392.74</td><td>$775.00</td><td>$825.00</td></tr>
                <tr><td>South Korea</td><td>$572.54</td><td>$732.29</td><td>$516.27</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Japan</td><td>$688.00</td><td>$506.06</td><td>$437.32</td></tr>
                <tr><td>United States</td><td>$550.00</td><td>$559.97</td><td>$500.00</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Italy</td><td>$600.00</td><td>$590.99</td><td>$479.85</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Canada</td><td>$579.80</td><td>$890.00</td><td>$600.00</td></tr>
            </table>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <h3>Strategic Sourcing Recommendation</h3>
                <p><strong>Buy Garmin in Hong Kong and Japan</strong> (${geo_spread['Garmin']:.0f} below global mean) and <strong>resell in Australia or South Korea</strong> where Garmin commands a 25-40% premium. For Apple, the US market offers the lowest entry point at $550 median — consider bulk acquisition from the US for international resale.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 4: Case Size Analysis -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>4. Case Size & Price Premium Analysis</h2>

            <div class="two-col">
                <div>
                    <h3>Case Size Statistics (mm)</h3>
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <tr><th>Brand</th><th>Mean</th><th>Median</th><th>Std Dev</th></tr>
                        <tr><td>Apple</td><td>46.2mm</td><td>46.0mm</td><td>3.1mm</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Garmin</td><td>45.5mm</td><td>45.5mm</td><td>3.9mm</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Samsung</td><td>45.0mm</td><td>46.0mm</td><td>2.4mm</td></tr>
                    </table>

                    <div class="insight-box">
                        <strong>Correlation Coefficient: {case_corr:.3f}</strong><br>
                        The near-zero correlation indicates that case size is a weak predictor of price in this dataset. Other factors (model tier, condition, included accessories) dominate price determination. However, within Garmin specifically, larger watches do show a slightly stronger correlation (0.107).
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['case_scatter']}" alt="Case Size vs Price">
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Practical Implication:</strong> Don't overstock large-case variants assuming a premium. While larger watches (47mm+) exist, they don't systematically command higher prices. Focus instead on model-specific demand and condition grades.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 5: Power Seller Behavior -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>5. Power Seller Behavior & Pricing Patterns</h2>

            <div class="two-col">
                <div>
                    <h3>Market Concentration Analysis</h3>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Total Unique Sellers</span>
                        <strong>{unique_sellers:,}</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Top 5% Seller Threshold</span>
                        <strong>{top_5_percent_cutoff} sellers</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Listings from Top 5%</span>
                        <strong>{power_seller_concentration:.1f}%</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Power Seller Avg Price</span>
                        <strong>${power_seller_avg_price:.2f}</strong>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-row">
                        <span>Regular Seller Avg Price</span>
                        <strong>${regular_seller_avg_price:.2f}</strong>
                    </div>

                    <div class="insight-box success">
                        <strong>Power sellers price 4.4% higher on average</strong>, suggesting they leverage reputation and inventory depth to command modest premiums rather than compete solely on price.
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['seller_box']}" alt="Power Seller Price Distribution">
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Brand Preference: Power Sellers vs Regular Sellers</h3>
            <table class="data-table">
                <tr><th>Brand</th><th>Power Sellers</th><th>Regular Sellers</th><th>Difference</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Apple</td><td>{power_seller_brand.get('Apple', 0):.1f}%</td><td>{regular_seller_brand.get('Apple', 0):.1f}%</td><td><span class="tag medium">{(power_seller_brand.get('Apple', 0) - regular_seller_brand.get('Apple', 0)):+.1f}%</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Garmin</td><td>{power_seller_brand.get('Garmin', 0):.1f}%</td><td>{regular_seller_brand.get('Garmin', 0):.1f}%</td><td><span class="tag high">{(power_seller_brand.get('Garmin', 0) - regular_seller_brand.get('Garmin', 0)):+.1f}%</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Samsung</td><td>{power_seller_brand.get('Samsung', 0):.1f}%</td><td>{regular_seller_brand.get('Samsung', 0):.1f}%</td><td><span class="tag high">{(power_seller_brand.get('Samsung', 0) - regular_seller_brand.get('Samsung', 0)):+.1f}%</span></td></tr>
            </table>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Strategic Insight:</strong> Power sellers are significantly over-indexed on Garmin (+27.9 pp) and Samsung (+7.2 pp) compared to regular sellers, while heavily under-indexed on Apple (-35.8 pp). This suggests experienced sellers recognize Garmin and Samsung offer better margin potential.
            </div>

            <h3>Condition Mix: Power Sellers vs Regular Sellers</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['condition']}" alt="Condition Distribution by Brand">
            </div>

            <p>Power sellers maintain a higher proportion of New inventory (53.0%) compared to the overall market. This focus on new and open-box items enables better quality control and supports the premium pricing they command.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 6: Strategic Recommendations -->
        <div class="section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #f8f9fa 0%, #e9ecef 100%);">
            <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>1. Prioritize Garmin for Margin-Focused Sourcing</h4>
                <p><strong>Finding:</strong> Garmin commands the highest median price (${median_by_brand["Garmin"]:.2f}) with the widest geographic spread (${geo_spread['Garmin']:.0f}). Power sellers disproportionately favor Garmin (+27.9 pp vs regular sellers).<br><br>
                <strong>Action:</strong> Build Garmin-focused sourcing pipelines. Target acquisition in Japan and Hong Kong where Garmin prices run 13-15% below global mean. Resell into Australia or South Korea where premiums reach 25-40%.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>2. Exploit Geographic Arbitrage — Focus on Apple</h4>
                <p><strong>Finding:</strong> Apple shows a $450 geographic spread with the US market offering the lowest median entry point ($550) and Australia the highest ($792).<br><br>
                <strong>Action:</strong> Source Apple Watch units from the US market en masse and resell internationally. The 44% price differential between US and Australian Apple listings represents pure arbitrage opportunity.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>3. Condition is King — Invest in New/Open Box Inventory</h4>
                <p><strong>Finding:</strong> Samsung commands an 18.2% new-vs-used premium, Apple 8.2%, and Garmin 4.7%. Power sellers maintain 53% new inventory and achieve 4.4% higher average prices.<br><br>
                <strong>Action:</strong> Prioritize sourcing new and open-box items, particularly Samsung. The condition premium significantly outweighs case size or color variations. Quality-certification partnerships with refurbishers can unlock consistent margins.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>4. Partner with Power Sellers for Inventory Scale</h4>
                <p><strong>Finding:</strong> The top 5% of sellers control {power_seller_concentration:.1f}% of listings and price 4.4% higher. They prefer Garmin and Samsung, and focus on new/open-box condition.<br><br>
                <strong>Action:</strong> Establish reseller partnerships with high-volume sellers. Their preference for Garmin/Samsung suggests inventory access to these brands at favorable terms. A consignment or volume-purchase agreement could provide consistent stock at predictable costs.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="recommendation">
                <h4>5. Don't Overweight Case Size — Focus on Model Tier</h4>
                <p><strong>Finding:</strong> Case size correlation with price is essentially zero (r={case_corr:.3f}). Standard deviation in case size within brands is ~3mm, meaning most listings cluster around 45-46mm regardless of price.<br><br>
                <strong>Action:</strong> De-prioritize case size in procurement decisions. Instead, focus on model tier (Ultra vs Series), included accessories, and condition grade — these factors drive 80%+ of price variance.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <h3>Summary: Top 3 Action Items</h3>
                <ol style="margin-top: 10px;">
                    <li style="margin: 10px 0;"><strong>Buy Garmin in Japan/Hong Kong → Sell in Australia/South Korea</strong> (25-40% margin potential)</li>
                    <li style="margin: 10px 0;"><strong>Source Apple from US → Resell internationally</strong> (44% price differential)</li>
                    <li style="margin: 10px 0;"><strong>Prioritize New/Open Box Samsung and Garmin</strong> (highest condition premium)</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div style="text-align: center; padding: 40px 20px; color: #666; font-size: 0.9rem;">
            <p>Report generated from analysis of {total_listings:,} smartwatch resale listings</p>
            <p>Data source: Kaggle Global Smartwatch Marketplace Insights 2026</p>
            <p>Q1 2026 | For E-Commerce Strategy Analyst audience</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>'''

